WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.117 --> 00:00:03.450
(foot steps)

00:00:05.203 --> 00:00:07.453
(clicking)

00:00:11.093 --> 00:00:14.676
(light instrumental music)

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.340
- To me, diversity means inclusion,

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.570
and when I say that I mean full inclusion.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.210
Because with full inclusion,

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.290
you're gonna get the best
quality of output from

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:34.990
anything that you're dealing with.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.530
- A friend of mine says, “You know Michael,

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.430
you weren't the first
homosexual at P&amp;G,

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:42.000
but you sure did put a public
face to it in the ‘80s.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.110
And it's true.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:44.803
You know, I think that description's right.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.830
I look back on this whole thing,

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:50.323
I think, God, you are one crazy queen.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.539
What drove me?

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:57.539
I'm a fighter, and, um, certainly
the time that I've been gay,

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.828
it has not been easy.

00:00:59.828 --> 00:01:01.174
- [Man] Get rid of 'em!

00:01:01.174 --> 00:01:02.670
- [Man] Yeah, get rid of 'em!

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.130
(cheering)

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:05.337
- [Group] Deport gays.

00:01:05.337 --> 00:01:06.880
Deport gays.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.387
Deport them.

00:01:08.387 --> 00:01:10.418
(people screaming)

00:01:10.418 --> 00:01:11.984
- Don't make me fight you.

00:01:11.984 --> 00:01:14.005
- [Man] We die, they do nothing.

00:01:14.005 --> 00:01:15.431
We die, they do nothing.

00:01:15.431 --> 00:01:16.606
(chanting)

00:01:16.606 --> 00:01:18.020
- If you don't know the history,

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:19.140
you're gonna repeat,

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.100
you know the famous line,

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.480
you're gonna repeat it.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.373
So I think that that's the key.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.400
It's important to understand the struggle

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.763
because the truth is, we're
always in the struggle.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.850
- Feel the power of this moment

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.010
and carry the message to the Capitol

00:01:36.161 --> 00:01:39.500
that we will have full human rights,

00:01:41.504 --> 00:01:44.563
civil rights for lesbian and gay people.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.173
And we will not be denied.

00:01:51.423 --> 00:01:54.423
(suspenseful music)

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.495
- [Man] OK, marker.

00:02:02.495 --> 00:02:04.390
- So someone came to me about

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.090
six months ago and they said, “Hey,

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.260
“you know, the 25th anniversary of P&amp;G

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:11.970
“including sexual orientation into

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:13.260
“its diversity statement's coming,

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.560
“and we'd like to make a big deal of it.”

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.020
I started thinking about it.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.500
Over the past 25 years we've made

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.270
an incredible amount of
progress towards inclusion,

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:24.250
but our current times remind
us it can be lost very quickly.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.093
And so a little light
bulb went off in my head.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.360
I thought, well, something
had to happen in 1992.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.530
Companies just don't make that shift,

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.310
as particularly at that time.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.310
I reached out to the LGBT employees

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:41.470
and asked, “Does anybody
remember what happened?”

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.970
One person wrote back and said,

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:45.370
“I think it had something to do with

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.500
“a mouthwash called Peridex.”

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:50.010
I called our archivist and said, “Peridex.”

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:50.843
“What is it?”

00:02:50.843 --> 00:02:53.280
“How would it possibly connect?”

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:54.890
They came back and they
said, “Well, we owned it.”

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:55.910
“We can't find any connection;

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:57.300
“we don't have much on it at all.”

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.460
So, a dead end.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:02:59.530
I emailed back again:

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.510
“Who knows something about Peridex?”

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:04.066
And one guy wrote back
and said, a friend of mine

00:03:04.066 --> 00:03:06.494
may have been involved in it;

00:03:06.494 --> 00:03:08.303
his name is Michael Chanak.

00:03:10.319 --> 00:03:12.110
(click)

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:13.963
- Oh boy, Cincinnati in the ‘80s.

00:03:14.874 --> 00:03:17.290
(sighs) I got there in '78.

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:21.044
Conservative, I mean, you know,
(chuckles) Cincinnati's roots,

00:03:21.044 --> 00:03:23.360
yeah, it's really a large cowtown.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.280
- If the world were coming to an end,

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:26.473
you'd wanna be in Cincinnati,

00:03:26.473 --> 00:03:28.800
because it wouldn't happen
here for seven years

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.510
after it happened everywhere else.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.547
Progress and moving things forward

00:03:32.547 --> 00:03:36.280
wasn't something that the community

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.332
as a whole seemed interested in.

00:03:38.332 --> 00:03:39.740
(whistling and cheering)

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:44.587
- I didn't go to Pride until about '85,

00:03:44.587 --> 00:03:48.690
because going to Pride
was in fact coming out.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:50.410
You know, that's like going to the cotillion,

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:51.810
your coming out party.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.820
- [Reporter] The parade
wound on for hours.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:57.180
A spectacle even Barnum
and Bailey might envy.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.320
- Because there was so much discrimination,

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:00.740
and there were so many problems,

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.020
you had to make friendships,

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:03.250
and they had to be good friendships.

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:05.195
And so I met some of the old-timers

00:04:05.195 --> 00:04:08.593
and got involved in
different political groups.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.290
I started at P&amp;G in April of '85,

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.180
and I really was a lab tech in

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.483
the old-fashioned sense of the world.

00:04:16.483 --> 00:04:18.420
I was an A and T person,

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.630
administrative technical.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:22.370
You know, there's a lot of things
in the workplace then,

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:23.250
everything you saw …  

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.460
There was a lot of homophobia,

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:26.830
there was a lot of sexism,

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:28.570
but that's how most people thought then.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:31.610
It was kind of a risky thing
being known as gay then.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:35.420
I mean, in those days it was kind of a
“friend of a friend” sort of thing,

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:37.203
like an old secret society.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:39.740
- It was isolating

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.110
because we couldn't talk to each other.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:41.943
There was that fear,

00:04:41.943 --> 00:04:42.990
there was that isolation.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.040
And then the rest of the
people you could talk to,

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.721
there was potential judgment.

00:04:46.721 --> 00:04:50.050
- I went to that Pride Parade in '86.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:51.950
I ran into my friend, Bob McNee.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:52.783
I liked Bob,

00:04:52.783 --> 00:04:53.700
it was great to see him,

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.220
it was a celebration.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:56.770
In those days you didn't see gay people,

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:57.603
you know what I mean?

00:04:57.603 --> 00:04:59.900
Two hundred people in a town
the size of Cincinnati,

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:01.670
gay pride 1986.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.460
These are like your
brothers and sisters here,

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:04.913
this is a homecoming week.

00:05:06.537 --> 00:05:09.760
(light instrumental music)

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:10.600
Bob and I kissed.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:13.180
And when the local TV station caught that,

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:16.000
it was on Saturday and
it was on Sunday night.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.363
And then it was even damn on Monday night.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:22.040
And that was really my coming out at work.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.080
A lot of people stopped by that morning,

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.310
about 30 people really,

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.670
and the message was clearly: You
have to be careful, Michael.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:30.173
You have to be careful.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:32.760
After being in that picture with

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.250
Bob McNee at the Pride thing,

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:35.623
I think something snapped.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.840
You just finally say
to yourself, screw it.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.220
I was the authentic Michael,

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:42.593
I was gonna be who I was.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:45.850
It's either gonna take you
or you're gonna take it.

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:47.390
I had made up my mind.

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:49.943
- And our guest today is Mike Chanak.

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:54.889
Noted interviewer and
leader in the gay community.

00:05:54.889 --> 00:05:58.760
- [Delaine] He was
willing to be an outlier.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:00.670
- [Ed] He wanted change for the better.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:02.690
He was willing to put it on the line,

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:04.440
he didn't sugarcoat things.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.860
- I've often been a target of a lot

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:08.624
of nasty stuff over the years.

00:06:08.624 --> 00:06:11.830
I'm not here to justify or not justify it,

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:14.360
but I do feel that I've heard

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.381
a lot of stuff about a lot of people.

00:06:16.381 --> 00:06:19.150
And I think it's our
own hatred of ourselves

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:21.930
that keeps us at each other so much.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:24.622
And we're so smart and yet so willing

00:06:24.622 --> 00:06:27.530
to believe some very strange things.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:28.743
And sometimes that's disappointing.

00:06:28.743 --> 00:06:30.100
I guess we all come out,

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:31.030
we look for community.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:34.250
And we find out that that's
a very difficult concept.

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:37.410
I had my debut in June of '86

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:39.130
on the local TV station.

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:40.250
And by the end of the year,

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:43.093
I'm transferred to the Peridex brand.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.430
Once I understood what
they were trying to do,

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:50.560
I thought, well, I got
something to offer here.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.010
- I was 23 years old and they put me on

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:54.760
this tiny little brand called Peridex

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.560
that was on no one's radar screen.

00:06:57.560 --> 00:06:59.430
It's a prescription mouthwash

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:01.780
and it was prescribed by dentists,

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:03.650
oral surgeons and periodontists

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:06.682
with an FDA indication for gingivitis.

00:07:06.682 --> 00:07:10.260
We started seeing market share for Peridex

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:13.440
really going up in San
Francisco and Boston

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.070
in ways that were kinda out of proportion.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:17.680
- [Reporter] Center for Disease Control

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:22.680
says nationwide AIDS has struck
1,641, mostly homosexuals.

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:26.262
Six hundred and forty-four people have died
from the mystery disease.

00:07:26.262 --> 00:07:29.300
- [Man] There have been doctors
all over the United States

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:32.850
who have, if not actually refused
to see patients with AIDS,

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:34.950
certainly been very unwilling to do so.

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.770
- [Michael] HIV started
out as being called GRID,

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:40.060
Gay Related Immune Deficiency,

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:42.510
and then subsequently, it became more

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:43.670
generally known as AIDS.

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:46.336
But by the time people
were aware of what it was,

00:07:46.336 --> 00:07:48.450
it was too late because there weren't

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:49.663
effective remedies then.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:54.040
- Procter and Gamble
really found this niche

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:57.170
with Peridex being used
by people with AIDS.

00:07:57.170 --> 00:07:59.580
With a pharmaceutical, you have to

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.150
be able to market with whatever

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:03.810
it's indicated for through the FDA.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:05.560
So we didn't have an indication

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:08.240
for what was called oral candidiasis,

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.660
it's basically known as thrush.

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:13.930
And many people with AIDS
would struggle with that.

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:16.086
Quality of life in terms of nutrition,

00:08:16.086 --> 00:08:17.490
you know, swallowing,

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:19.730
just that basic “what made a good day

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:21.730
or a bad day” became more prominent.

00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:23.120
- [Woman] The cause of the acquired

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:25.800
immune deficiency syndrome, or AIDS,

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.950
is unknown and there is no known cure.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:31.340
- You had a lifespan of six months,

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:33.440
maybe 18 months if you were lucky.

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:34.320
If you can't eat,

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:35.240
you die quicker,

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:36.250
that's the bottom line.

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:37.820
And if there's something that was better

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:39.756
than the current treatments,

00:08:39.756 --> 00:08:42.430
then it was worth pursuing.

00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:44.420
- We were doing qualitative research

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:47.470
in the form of focus
groups and one on ones

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:49.770
and going to doctors
that were prescribing it.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:53.200
It was a way for us to both learn more,

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:55.030
but also just be in community

00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:57.400
and have a conversation with 30 people

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:00.200
in a church basement
or a community center.

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:02.150
They were able to share their experience

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:03.467
and how it worked or why it worked.

00:09:03.467 --> 00:09:06.890
If we are marketing this product

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:08.620
whether indirectly or directly

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:11.130
to people with AIDS and then affecting

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:14.050
the larger LGBT community,

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:17.370
then we need to hold ourself
to the same standard.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:19.190
- There was this disconnect.

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:20.220
You know, they wanna do the science,

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:21.360
they wanna do the marketing,

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:22.930
but they didn't quite get,

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:24.410
because they weren't gay,

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:26.920
the piece that these are people's lives,

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:28.830
and these people are right here,

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:30.403
even inside of this company.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:33.060
The first mistake you can make

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:34.890
when it comes to dealing with queens,

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:36.090
if you're gonna sell them something,

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:37.490
you better damn well claim them.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:39.200
I had to say that message many,

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:42.690
many, many, many times before I finally

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:45.812
found a few people that said, “You know,
you're right.”

00:09:45.812 --> 00:09:47.860
- Michael knocked on my door,

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:48.693
came in,

00:09:48.693 --> 00:09:50.870
I mean, he's a very affable guy.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:54.790
He told me that things were not good

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:58.943
for the LGBT community in P&amp;G.

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:01.710
- There was always
these programs about it,

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:04.030
even then there were
programs about inclusion.

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:06.340
But those were for Asians,

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:08.120
those were for Hispanics,

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:10.510
and I pointed out well, where are the gays?

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:11.470
Where are the lesbians?

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:13.720
I went and had these face to face talks,

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:16.110
And I finally got it out of the guy.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:17.460
Well, we didn't include them,

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:18.410
we don't have to include them,

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:20.349
we're not gonna include them.

00:10:20.349 --> 00:10:23.140
But that's the kind of atmosphere it was.

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:25.060
They were not ready to embrace that,

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:26.690
they were not wanting to embrace it.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:30.630
- I didn't realize that he
was getting kind of harassed,

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:35.140
getting some letters that
were not very pleasant.

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:37.010
- This one was marked confidential,

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:39.800
open at a time you have
30 minutes to one hour.

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:41.843
He had a specific interval of time.

00:10:41.843 --> 00:10:44.930
And this is the Romans’ Map to Heaven,

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:48.810
the truthful bible road to
heaven from The Book of Romans.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:52.190
Mike, I was told about
what some saw on TV.

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:53.970
I don't judge you,

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:55.291
I know you have torment,

00:10:55.291 --> 00:10:59.060
but my Lord still heals,
and he cares for you.

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:02.880
And he will make a way from
which there appears no way out.

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:04.330
Have faith.

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:07.679
And my all time favorite with a cartoon,

00:11:07.679 --> 00:11:10.530
gay but not happy.

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:12.103
We understand, we've been there,

00:11:12.103 --> 00:11:14.750
but Jesus has set us free.

00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:17.670
God makes a way when there is no way.

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:20.870
I'd walk down the hall and get
called “faggot” by some folks.

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:23.250
That's the work environment.

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:26.570
- The issue that Michael
was most concerned about

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:30.050
was seeing the words sexual orientation

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:31.865
right alongside all the other

00:11:31.865 --> 00:11:35.430
legal prohibitions against discrimination

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.963
in the EEO policy statement.

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.920
P&amp;G wanted to be not only lawful,

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:44.460
but doing the right thing.

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:48.630
But doing the right thing
went beyond the law.

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:51.280
Over the next four years,
we tried every year,

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:53.170
we'd run it up the flagpole.

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:56.430
- I was constantly gathering information,

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:59.390
what other companies were
doing as far as the gay issue,

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:01.418
the impact of gays in the marketplace,

00:12:01.418 --> 00:12:03.973
you know, what this really represented to them.

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:07.410
- The first recommendation we wrote

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:12.410
was just to put it in and talk
about what others were doing,

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:16.280
how diversity was important
and if we were going to say it,

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:17.273
we had to live it.

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:20.583
I don't think it got
all the way to the CEO,

00:12:20.583 --> 00:12:24.270
I don't think it got out of
our department. (chuckles)

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:28.890
- To change the policy required
a business justification.

00:12:28.890 --> 00:12:31.370
- Peridex was a prescription mouthwash

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:33.780
that allowed us to build a business case

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:37.313
that, you know, in my words, were
just gonna tip the scale.

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:40.350
This is what we need to do as a brand,

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:41.440
as a business,

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:44.083
and absolutely for our own employees.

00:12:44.083 --> 00:12:46.333
(printing)

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:50.663
- [Michael] Peridex was the key,

00:12:52.437 --> 00:12:55.090
but it wasn't enough.

00:12:55.090 --> 00:12:57.340
(chanting)

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:00.660
- This is what we were told.

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:04.410
Because it was not yet part
of the federal mandate,

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:06.860
why would we go further?

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:10.680
I can see Michael and hear his words now,

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:14.010
tell me again that the EEO has gone up

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:16.380
and that we were not included.

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:19.027
- How much energy are you
going to invest of your soul

00:13:19.027 --> 00:13:23.820
to work at a company that
doesn't support who you are?

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:27.690
- P&amp;G was talking about
diversity on the one hand

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:31.060
and how diversity was the
strength of the company,

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:32.110
it just wasn't happening.

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:33.490
- It wasn't in their mindset,

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:36.680
it wasn't in their wheel house of reality.

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:37.890
It's not that their hateful people,

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:39.640
it's just outside of their sphere.

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:40.720
- When you're not gay,

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:41.660
you're not a woman,

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:43.600
you're not a person of color,

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:46.600
you can sit back and say, “You know, of
course we don't discriminate.”

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:49.330
And the fact is, they're
not in that position

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:51.590
to experience discrimination.

00:13:51.590 --> 00:13:54.640
- P&amp;G wanted to make sure no issues

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:57.160
were going to arise; is there going to be

00:13:57.160 --> 00:14:00.480
in a backlash from our consumers,

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:02.070
from other employees,

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:03.980
from you name it?

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:06.830
- [Michael] Every attempt we
had to make more of a case.

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:08.640
- [Lyn] They were still afraid to do it.

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:09.970
- [Michael] So back we went again.

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:11.060
- [Lyn] Year after year … after year.

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:11.895
- [Michael] And again.

00:14:11.895 --> 00:14:14.430
- Which seemed like an eternity.

00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:16.150
- It was either change or be changed.

00:14:16.150 --> 00:14:19.791
- [Lyn] In no way was Michael
Chanak going to back away.

00:14:19.791 --> 00:14:22.510
- [Michael] Months would go by and
then simply you'd hear a “no.”

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:24.460
- So we had to kick it up a notch.

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:25.610
- There's a saying in the bible about

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:27.030
having the faith of a mustard seed.

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:28.990
Well, here's my faith:

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:30.080
“It will change.”

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:34.100
- I thought that we needed to
get our legal ducks in a row.

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:36.625
Tom was able to do that.

00:14:36.625 --> 00:14:39.427
- Lyn said that I think
it'll carry more weight

00:14:39.427 --> 00:14:43.890
if the recommendation originates
in the legal division.

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:44.960
It was important to me,

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:46.090
I was a gay man.

00:14:46.090 --> 00:14:47.870
I had invested interest.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:50.450
My goal was to get it approved

00:14:50.450 --> 00:14:52.510
and the other benefits
would flow afterwards,

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:54.660
but let's get the thing in place first.

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:56.810
- We had to rein in the passion,

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:58.130
just stick with the facts.

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:01.528
- He had one shot at making this happen.

00:15:01.528 --> 00:15:03.480
- There are cities and schools

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:05.950
that are requiring us not to discriminate

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:07.850
on the basis of sexual orientation.

00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:09.400
From a business standpoint,

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:11.040
we're hurting ourselves.

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:12.780
- [Lyn] That was the perfect hook

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:15.170
to give us more ammunition.

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:17.890
- If we're not matching our competitors,

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:20.410
we were probably losing some good people

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:21.810
that we shouldn't have been losing.

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:24.630
- You have to work up that command chain

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:26.210
and you have to have people that believe

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:27.160
it's the right thing to do.

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:29.870
And there has to be reasons
that it's the right thing to do.

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:31.723
There has to be stats and figures.

00:15:31.723 --> 00:15:34.490
And what is the consequence of not doing it?

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:37.440
This is a matter of
survival for our community.

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:39.240
- [Tom] It went up through the ranks,

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:40.283
it went up quickly.

00:15:41.150 --> 00:15:45.675
I saw it when I came back and
there were three just “agreeds”

00:15:45.675 --> 00:15:49.290
and then the most important
two letters of all, “O-K.”

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:52.643
And it had E-L-A after it; and
that was Mr. Artzt — Ed Artzt.

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:54.933
It was a done deal.

00:15:56.039 --> 00:15:58.539
(light music)

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:03.770
- September 15, 1992, for those of us

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:05.330
that had touched it in any way,

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:08.130
for those us that were involved
in diversity and inclusion,

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:09.530
it was just a thrilling day.

00:16:09.530 --> 00:16:11.110
There was kind of like a silent

00:16:11.110 --> 00:16:13.373
smile on our faces that whole day.

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:18.420
- You know, you have that
moment where you say “oh, my God,”

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:19.550
maybe you didn't really think

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:22.448
it was gonna happen and it did, finally.

00:16:22.448 --> 00:16:25.083
It's just a lot to take in.

00:16:26.095 --> 00:16:29.682
- [Lyn] (laughing) Oh, my goodness.

00:16:29.682 --> 00:16:30.515
- [Mike] Let me see you.

00:16:30.515 --> 00:16:31.872
- Oh, look at you.

00:16:31.872 --> 00:16:33.273
You look like a teenager,

00:16:33.273 --> 00:16:35.600
what have you done?

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:36.900
- Words matter.

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:38.310
Policies do matter.

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:40.880
Making something legitimate, legal,

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:42.979
taking a stand matters.

00:16:42.979 --> 00:16:44.650
- [Tom] We didn't get everything right

00:16:44.650 --> 00:16:46.670
and we certainly weren't the first,

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:48.930
but what's important
is that we got it done.

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:50.660
And the lessons that we learned then,

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:53.340
makes it so much easier to go forward.

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:56.370
- All of the work that Michael and Anne

00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:57.950
and Lyn and Tom did just to get

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:01.633
those two words into the EEO statement,

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:05.028
those two words had a profound impact.

00:17:05.028 --> 00:17:07.270
Maybe not in the day after,

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:09.057
but in the weeks after,
and the months after,

00:17:09.057 --> 00:17:10.970
and in the years after,

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:12.460
they were the start of everything.

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:14.160
- Procter's come to understand

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:17.349
that if you can't bring your
whole self to the workplace,

00:17:17.349 --> 00:17:20.280
you're not gonna do an effective job.

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:21.200
You're hiding something,

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:23.933
you're holding something
back of who you are.

00:17:25.063 --> 00:17:26.530
(cheering)

00:17:26.530 --> 00:17:29.640
It's always important
to understand the past,

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:30.920
because it's instructive,

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:32.750
'cause you learn from the techniques,

00:17:32.750 --> 00:17:33.790
what the issues were,

00:17:33.790 --> 00:17:35.030
how they were dealt with.

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:37.966
That gives you a clue to the future.

00:17:37.966 --> 00:17:40.203
The struggle isn't over now.

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:46.050
- We didn't think about
there being an LGBT community

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:48.920
as I probably should have in hindsight,

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:52.000
as being a factor that could make a person

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:53.480
either feel welcome or not,

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:54.500
safe or not.

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:57.020
- [Brent] When this
started, there was no way

00:17:57.020 --> 00:17:59.720
that they knew what this would turn into.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:02.290
I mean, this created a path for
domestic partner benefits,

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:05.050
it created a path for
transgender benefits,

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:07.410
it created a path that, you know, ultimately

00:18:07.410 --> 00:18:09.350
has led to marriage equality.

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:11.810
- We're still on this path, this journey,

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:13.250
of having an environment where everybody

00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:15.970
can be who they are
and what they wanna be.

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:17.650
It's only by doing that
that we make progress

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:19.650
as a company or as a society.

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:21.349
- It's those Michaels of the world

00:18:21.349 --> 00:18:25.217
that help to create incredible change.

00:18:25.217 --> 00:18:28.140
(light music)

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:30.023
- [Michael] It'll change
if you believe it'll change

00:18:30.023 --> 00:18:31.483
and you work for it.

00:18:33.140 --> 00:18:34.373
That's my philosophy.

00:18:35.395 --> 00:18:37.978
(light music)

